lephants you know  they're very big and they can rip up  trees and do a lot of damage they're  very conspicuous but if you really want  to know what shapes the terrain and  what's really in charge from day to day  in this jungle it's all the little  flying and stinging creatures it's the  worms and the bugs and the nematodes and  the algae and the spiders all these  little things that are going on about  their business and no one controls that  the voters don't control it they have  some influence politicians don't control  it they have some influence but mostly  the jungle runs itself  that's what Washington's become so what  does that mean well it means a lot of  things the  first thing it means is when I call my  book governments end I mean end in a  very specific sense not that it comes to  an end but that it's reached a kind of  stable equilibrium a destination point  as it were a point from which it's very  hard to turn back a point from which you  can't unnie volve and the basic meaning  of that is that if you want to know how  government will look very roughly for  the foreseeable future look at it right  now that isn't to say it won't change  very significantly you might have more  spending on defense and more on Social  Security as people get older the  contours of the jungle change but if  you're the sort of person who dreams of  some big comprehensive rationalizing  reform of government you're going to be  disappointed if you're a conservative  who wants to make government a small  town again as the gold water rights and  Reaganites and gingrich heights all  wanted to do it's time to leave the  pumpkin patch and go home no one has the  power to do that similarly if you're a  liberal and you want to go back to the  heyday of the Great Society or FDR make  America's government much more like  Sweden's you can't do that either  the jungle won't let you do it it means  from now on we're talking about a  situation which is on the one hand very  stable which is good but on the other  hand you can only really change it on  the margins if it lets accept the  premise that government slowly becomes  the Washington slowly becomes less  effective in what it's able to do  discuss if you can three recent reform  initiatives that have been a topic of a  lot of discussion in Washington welfare  reform telecommunications reform and  campaign finance reform those three are  all very different one of the things I  try to emphasize in the book is you know  people always hear this and they think  what a gloom and doom but what pessimism  you know what a cheerless wretched view  of the world this author takes you don't  seem like a cheerless wretched well I'm  not and in fact fundamentally I argue  that this is in many ways a quite  optimistic book that what's happened is  over the last 20 or 30 years voters  developed false expectations of how much  you could do overnight and they became  continually frustrated when they voted  for Paula to  who promised huge reforms like Gingrich  or Clinton for that matter and who then  couldn't deliver and that voters are now  wising up to this they're becoming  reconciled to the fact that you can only  change it on the margins on the one hand  but on the other hand and this is very  important the margins matter they matter  a lot if you can pass a welfare reform  you don't change the whole government  overnight but you do overhaul a very  significant program and you do it in a  way they can do a lot of benefit one of  the great things we did in Washington is  in the 70s we deregulated the  transportation industry this had an  enormous ly beneficial effect on a whole  sector of the economy that was something  that Jimmy Carter did for which he  deserves a lot of credit tax reform in  the 80s it didn't last of course because  we cleaned up the tax code and then all  the little groups went back to building  all their tax loopholes back in but  nonetheless for a while there we were  able to sweep it clean we were able to  clean house a very significant and  important reform telecommunications I  would say was has been less important so  far and I know less about it but the  point is that although you can't sort of  take the whole government in your hands  and reform it from the top down you can  go after important key programs and make  them better than that that's what the  game is all about and as the voters  begin to understand that that's the  definition of success not some huge area  forum but specific real reforms of  program that will become easier to do  and that those marginal changes will  become easier and that's very important  you dedicate your book to Mansur Olson  you write to the memory of Mansur Olson  teacher who was Mansur Olson an  economist University of Maryland he died  almost exactly two years ago in 1998  Mansur Olson wrote two extremely  important books which greatly influenced  my thinking in this book the first was  called the logic of collective action  published in 1965 and the second was  called the rise and decline of great  nations Olson argued from an economists  theoretical point of view that any  stable society will tend to accumulate  interest groups  in groups for collective action over  time and that in any society that's free  basically any democracy in which people  can form groups gradually they will he  also pointed out that they're much  harder to get rid of than to form and  thus it's Olson who gives us this idea  of a direction of time in society where  other things being equal you get this  accumulation and build-up this is one of  the central tenets of your book it seems  absolutely largely built on the  intellectual structure that did man sir  Olson put in place in his economic  thinking what he did not do was apply  that thinking to government he was  interested in the role of interest  groups in the economy so he was looking  at cartels he was looking at labor  unions and he was looking at the  economic effect of subsidies and what  could happen to an economy as they  accumulated over time I took that idea  and said now wait a minute the same  process happens to government itself as  it becomes more and more cluttered with  these groups that demand things and that  never go away you mentioned two books  what what's the second book by Olson  that played a role in your evolution of  thinking it's called the rise and  decline of nations if I have been the  title correct it was published in 1982  and that's the book where he takes the  basic insight about the accumulation of  groups and says this could actually help  explain why societies why countries like  Britain through the 50s and 60s seemed  to age and grow old and why it is that  after an enormous transforming  catastrophe like a totalitarian rule  which of course is bad or a great war  why they seem to bounce back and and  come back with additional vigor as  Germany and Japan did after World War  two he said it's because you sweep away  the accumulation of groups and you can  build fresh what are the policy  implications of that theory well Olson  himself was of two minds about it he  liked to say that he was optimistic  every other day of the week he believes  that the key to this was was educating  the public he believed that the more  people understood about this process  that was altering society over time the  more they'd be able to do about it so he  put great hope in education  and in some sense to a limited extent I  follow him in that and that's why I  wrote this book we've had a largely  pathological relationship between the  American public in the American  government over the last twenty to  thirty years where people expect too  much and are constantly disappointed and  I think to the extent that people learn  what's going on here learn why just  trying to elect a McCain who will claim  to drive a bulldozer through all the  lobbies in Washington but that's never  going to work that they'll begin to  focus on more productive strategies that  may be a little less glamorous but will  be more effective and in fact I think  that may be starting to happen in this  year's election is there a certain magic  do you think - the word that we've heard  a lot and that we you and I have used  here reform yeah there's a lot of magic  to it and of course we're all the  reformer with results in in this  election cycle why is that word so  powerful well you know people are  dissatisfied with the federal government  they were quite well satisfied with it  through Kennedy when you asked if they  trusted it to do the right thing  seventy-five percent of them said that  they did those figures began to decline  in the Vietnam days and have never  recovered the percentage saying that  they trust government to do the right  thing is now 20 to 30 very low people  don't really like what they're getting  from Washington they can live with it  but this gives I think reform a great  deal of allure and of course I'm a  reformer - I just argue you have to be  realistic about how much you can reform  in any given year but and you also have  to be realistic about the obstacles to  reform that you know it's one thing to  be for reform in the abstract we're all  for reform in the abstract but in order  to change anything in this city you have  to change some particular program and  that program is structured the way it is  because some particular group is  benefiting from it and because they've  given a lot of campaign money and  because there's a chairman of some  committee or someone from some state who  likes it that way it becomes very hard  to translate these area notions of  reform into the particulars of  governance you've been a journalist  covering Washington for some time now  let's get your thoughts if we can on I'm  gonna ask you about several industries  or activities and their role is as  either a productive element or an  obstacle  in this reform dynamic that you're  talking about let's begin with the media  well you know it's hard to answer that  in that way because a core argument of  my book as you know is that we're all  both on the one hand we're all trying to  reform things in our lives and in the  programs we work with to make them more  successful or efficient from our  personal point of view but on the other  hand we're also defending what we've got  my reform is your obstacle so I think  we're all some of both if you ask what's  been the role of the media in all of  this of course I'm a journalist and I'm  biased I tend to think that the media  actually is much like the voters that  we're not all that different from  anybody else that on the one hand we  tend to like reform in the abstract  we're always for reform in the abstract  but that on the other hand when a  president or a Newt Gingrich comes along  and says he's going to make some changes  in Medicare the first thing we do is  pick up our phones call the American  Association of Retired people and take  down the most inflammatory quote we can  possibly get and put it on the wires and  then write a lead about how President  Clinton's Medicare proposal ran into  trouble today when group XY and Z said  such-and-such so like everybody else in  this country I think we're on both sides  at once the legal profession you know  there you have an interesting case a lot  of what's happened in the last two years  by way of policy reform has come not  through the standards system but through  lawsuits filed by class action lawyers  plaintiffs lawyers often with the  assistance of governments as have  happened in tobacco cases and the gun  cases and if you ask them what they're  doing they'll say well Congress wasn't  doing its job so we had to help Congress  was so gridlocked and so bound out by  special interests that we had to go  around the process to get done what we  wanted to do now this it seems to me is  a cure that's worse than the disease the  founders didn't want it to be easy to  make change and the idea that you can go  around the democratic process and use an  undemocratic one in order to solve these  problems strikes me as exactly the wrong  sort of way for voters and politicians  to vent this frustration I think we're  going to have to work  through the system and change the system  where we can but we oughtn't to be  subverting it is the law then you have a  chart if I can this is from the chapter  again the parasite economy private suits  filed in the federal courts it's a chart  from 1950 all the way to the year 2000  with a bit of a break there and we see  what obviously looks like a steady  increase are you making a connection  then between the amount of litigation we  see in society and the a word that's  used disenfranchisement perhaps voter  from government government there's some  connection in the particular chart that  you refer to I'm making the broader  point that as societies age they tend to  devote more and more of their resources  to people who struggle to claim existing  resources it's called transfer seeking  it's called my lobbying to get more of  what you've got or my suing to get more  of what you've got and that this  transfer seeking activity although  everyone involved believes themselves to  be doing the right thing for themselves  and for the country it's actually very  expensive because there's so much cost  as people fight back and forth and I was  counting lawsuits and lawyers as one  very crude indicator we have that  suggest there's a lot more transfer  seeking industry going on in America  today than there was say 40 years ago I  don't want to dwell on this too much  because there's a lot more in the book  than just this but again returning to  this question of looking at industry's  role perhaps as either positive role or  obstacle what about think tanks think  tanks are to my mind not much of the  problem if at all now I say that perhaps  self-interested Lee because I'm a writer  in residence at the brookings  institution which is Washington's oldest  possibly largest think tank but ideas  are not like federal programs they come  and they go you can always put more  ideas on the table and the more the  better you know you have genuine  pluralism with ideas one of the good  thing that's happened in Washington I  think in the last 30 years or so has  been the great proliferation of points  of view in this city we now have think  tanks all the way from the far right  through libertarian Center all the way  to the far left putting up position  paper  making points-of-view known and I think  that that more information adds insight  to the system so my view is that the in  the ideas industry on for change you  have something that's really not very  much part of the problem I think Senator  Moynihan once referred to them as the  idea factories finally what about the  public relations industry well again you  know these things touch all of us I have  a sister in the public relations  industry and like everyone else they do  what they do if I could broaden your  your inquiry and answer it slightly more  broadly the PR industry is one facet of  one of the really important developments  in government's in the last 30 years  which has been the professionalization  of the lobbying class you know it used  to be  there weren't many lobbyists in this  town there are a few thousand they all  knew somebody and lobbying actually  meant you know you go and rub elbows  with some congressmen and you play golf  with them and maybe you slipped them a  little campaign money while you were at  it  that's that model is ancient history  lobbying today does mean you do a little  of that but much more than that it means  professionalized grassroots activism it  means you've got these enormous  specialty lines where all you have to do  is press a button into your national  headquarters and they'll generate phone  calls in any congressional district in  the country where you want to try to  swing a congressman's vote and the PR  industry is part of this  professionalization you now have image  shapers who will tell you exactly what  you need to do to save your program if  next year President Clinton decides to  put in his budget that he wants to  reduce the budget and you've got this  enormous infrastructure built up now  people who do this for a living that's  another thing that's never going away in  a democracy we're going to have to live  with that very efficient very  professional lobbying sector so if you  say I believe you say the government  that we have now is the government that  we essentially always will have and we  have this explosion in the number of  lobbying groups in the number of  collective interest organizations public  relations industry advances 10 20 years  from now what is what are the  implications for American governance  implication number one is that  fundamentally thing  won't look all that much different than  they do now though government will  always be changing for months a month  people are very busy in this town  changing things on a sort of microcosmic  level if the macrocosm won't look all  that much different but it also means I  think and here we get to the message of  what we can actually do about this I  think it creates possibilities for a  more effective reform style if you'll  allow me to go back to the yet another  medical metaphor suppose you're getting  older and you go to your doctor and you  say I'm old and I'm tired and I've got a  heart condition and here's how I'm  dealing with it I'm sitting on my sofa  complaining about getting old and twice  a year I go on a crash diet which  doesn't seem to work your doctors likely  to say that's not a very effective  approach to ageing gracefully and  successfully he's likely to say what you  need is a regular moderate program of  exercise you need to watch your diet you  don't ever expect to be young again you  know you can't sit on your sofa  bemoaning the loss of youth but you can  understand that you can age much more  gracefully and retain much more vitality  into old age if you keep at it if you  work slowly but surely on diet and  exercise and healthy habits and the same  is true of government I think if we  focus on incremental reforms of the sort  that we can do and have done  successfully for the last 30 years  government never gets young again but we  can keep it vital enough so that to a  first approximation it will do many of  the basic things we need to do little  right Social Security checks fairly well  we'll be able to work around it to a  considerable extent and in fact to an  extent we do that already with things  like charter schools and that all of  these things mean that none of this is a  disaster unless you think that the  current government is a complete  dysfunctional disaster it basically  means that although government will  continue to age and will never be young  and vigorous and extremely creative on  the other hand it will never be terribly  destructive and awful and will it be  able to keep it at least vigorous enough  to do the basic things we need to me  that's not so terrible so it's possible  for old democracies to age gracefully I  guess  I hope it is you know we're now the  oldest democracy in the world and that's  part of the reason we are where we are  and the good news is that we're doing  better than many other democracies that  are younger for instance we're doing a  much better job coping with our problems  than Japan is done because we're a more  open society a more open economy and we  have a more competitive government where  our states compete with each other and  with the federal government and  localities compete with the states and  all of those add some elements of  competition and flexibility which are on  our side and I'm optimistic that this  year is we see an electorate that's less  interested in kind of rabid government  bashing and more interested in sort of  pragmatic one step at a time candidates  like george w bush and an al gore that  we're seeing an electorate that's  becoming more willing to live with that  kind of incremental but nonetheless  useful kind of reform what is it like to  go from writing a column a weekly or do  you write a biweekly column i write a  biweekly column what's it like the  difference between writing a weekly or  bi-weekly column and a book boy you know  i write so many things in so many  different lengths that each piece has a  life of its own but a column of course  you look at what's going on that week  you look at what's in people's minds and  you try to try to address it and put it  in perspective and maybe bring a new  slant with the book you try to get  precisely above what's in people's minds  and you try to say wait a minute get  away from who's in the White House at  the moment get away from this notion of  gridlock just at present because the  Republicans have only a five or six seat  majority in the House who represented  think about the big picture look at the  long term and of course for a writer  there's there's no pleasure greater than  being able to stand really far back look  at the broad sweep of history and try to  make sense of it all I want if I can to  ask you a question about the language  that you employ throughout the book I'm  looking at page 24 and this is a  description again of mansur Olsen this  in the chapter mr. Olsen's planet I  wonder if you could just read a little  bit of this description of who he was  for us though he held a Harvard PhD in a  fancy title at the University of  Maryland even in his later years he  could still repair a tractor and he  midwestern matter-of-factness that made  his writing like his speech vigorous and  to the point yet an intellectual fire  burned in him he had a jumpy delighted  almost pick sheis excitability when he  talked about ideas he would ask visitors  to let him pace while he spoke and then  he prowled the way some people just  stick you late as though the movement  helped him form words the effect was  pleasently gnomish how much effort does  it take to create a description like  that I think it was John Kenneth  Galbraith or somebody who spoke of the  sense of spontaneity and natural  naturalness that comes in about the  sixteenth draft and I endorsed that  though sometimes I find that the first  draft is the best after all my advice  for a writer is revise revise revise  Jonathan Roush the book is government's  end why Washington stopped working it  was published by public affairs in 1999  thank you very much for coming in thank  you too a great pleasure
 

